[17592186088144 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot y=x,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186088144 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot y = x, y(0) = 1$\nSelect the correct options below. An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534782 17592186045417 false] [17592186088144 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $Q(x)=x.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (x/2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}/4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{x}{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\cdot y & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}) & =x{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4})\\text{d}x & =\\int x{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} & =\\int 2{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} & =2{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{2}/4} & =2{e}^{{x}^{2}/4}+C \\\\ y & =2+C{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =2+C{e}^{-{0}^{2}/4} \\\\ 1 & =2+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2-{e}^{-{x}^{2}/4}.\n$$" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186088861 1064 "We will now derive the formula for the integrating factor. \n\nConsider the first-order linear equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the left-hand side as the derivative of a product.\n\nTo do that, we multiply both sides of the equation by some function $I(x)$ that we wish to determine.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I(x)P(x)}y} = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe want the left-hand side to match the derivative of $y\\cdot I(x)$. But by the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr)\n=\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I'(x)}y}.\n$$\n\nSo, if we choose $I(x)$ so that\n\n$$\nI'(x) = I(x)P(x),\n$$\n\nthen the left-hand side becomes $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr).$\n\nDividing both sides of $I'(x) = I(x)P(x)$ by $I(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{I'(x)}{I(x)} = P(x).\n$$\n\nNext, integrating both sides with respect to $x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{I}^{'}(x)}{I(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}(I(x)))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(I(x)) & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{C}\\cdot {e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, because multiplying $I(x)$ by a nonzero constant does not change the method, we can choose $e^C = 1$ and define an integrating factor as\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" 13194139534782 17592186045417 false] [17592186088861 1064 "We will now derive the formula for the integrating factor. \n\nConsider the first-order linear equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the left-hand side as the derivative of a product.\n\nTo do that, we multiply both sides of the equation by some function $I(x)$ that we wish to determine.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I(x)P(x)}y} = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe want the left-hand side to match the derivative of $y\\cdot I(x)$. But by the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr)\n=\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I'(x)}y}.\n$$\n\nSo, if we choose $I(x)$ so that\n\n$$\nI'(x) = I(x)P(x),\n$$\n\nthen the left-hand side becomes $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr).$\n\nDividing both sides of $I'(x) = I(x)P(x)$ by $I(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{I'(x)}{I(x)} = P(x).\n$$\n\nNext, integrating both sides with respect to $x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{I}^{′}(x)}{I(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(I(x)))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(I(x)) & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{C}\\cdot {e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, because multiplying $I(x)$ by a nonzero constant does not change the method, we can choose $e^C = 1$ and define an integrating factor as\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186311587 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186311587 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139534782 17592186045417 false] [17592186435831 1080 "{e}^{-2x}" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435831 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435831 1083 #uuid "0249902f-f26e-5a08-91d2-72b3ea108e58" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435832 1080 "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x|)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435832 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435832 1083 #uuid "0b978ec0-c1dc-593e-85ec-c512eb8b8bba" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435833 1080 "-2x" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435833 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435833 1083 #uuid "12775c73-4492-5f55-9c3e-64f7ff76cc43" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435834 1080 "{e}^{-2x}(1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435834 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435834 1083 #uuid "1d921686-f16a-5dba-8a57-bf18cea65de9" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435835 1080 "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435835 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435835 1083 #uuid "1da376ec-fadc-58b2-9050-98c3a0e3289d" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435836 1080 "{x}^{4}" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435836 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435836 1083 #uuid "21b04f8f-b31f-5e1d-8a4e-18caced2e9c1" 13194139534782 17592186045417 true] [17592186435837 1080 "2{e}^{-